<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orléans</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cenabum, Aurelianum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Orléans is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the Gallic town of Cenabum, whose massacre of Roman merchants in 52 BCE began the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a>. Rebuilt by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> as Aurelianum, it became a Merovingian royal capital and a core town of the Capetian kings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a> relieved the English siege of the city in 1429.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orleans.jpg" alt="Orléans" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bmazerolles · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rue_Jeanne_dArc_Tramway_Orleans.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…separate groups across the Rhine in 406, and while Respendial's Alans saved the Vandals from defeat by the Franks, Goar went over to the Roman side and his followers were later settled near <b>Orléans</b> by the general Aetius; another group under Sambida was settled near Valence around 440.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Politically, the Milanese claim of the house of <b>Orléans</b> drew France into Italy and made the duchy the cockpit of the Italian Wars, while the Peace of Lodi and the Italic League of 1455 offered an early model of a balance of power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Palatinate suffered again in the Nine Years' War, when Louis XIV's troops, pressing the inheritance claims of Elizabeth Charlotte, duchess of <b>Orléans</b>, systematically burned Heidelberg, Mannheim, Speyer and Worms in 1689, and Heidelberg Castle was blown up in 1689 and 1693.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 451 they besieged <b>Orléans</b> until Flavius Aetius relieved it, and in 452 Attila sacked cities across northern Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Franco-Prussian War two Bavarian army corps took part in the Battle of Sedan, where Bavarian troops fought the bitter battle for the village of Bazeilles, and in the campaign around <b>Orléans</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Hugh's real power was modest: the royal domain was a narrow strip around Paris and <b>Orléans</b>, while the dukes of Normandy, Aquitaine and Burgundy and the counts of Flanders, Champagne and Toulouse owed only loose homage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
